lly, maps its fields, and sends it to FlexBIT. If delivery fails, it keeps the event and retries."},"11":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-9","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works","content":"Use the BESS example below to try the full flow. The same steps apply to other asset types."},"12":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-10","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#1-connect-your-device-to-a-flexbit-asset","content":"Open **Mappings**. Under **Device mappings**, select **Add device mapping**, enter your local device ID, and select the destination FlexBIT asset."},"13":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-11","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#1-connect-your-device-to-a-flexbit-asset","content":"For this example, use `demo-bess` as the local device ID and select a BESS asset from your site. This tells the adapter which FlexBIT asset should receive the device's measurements."},"14":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-12","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#1-connect-your-device-to-a-flexbit-asset","content":"The demo mappings shown here are included with the installation. Replace the example device mappings with your own asset selections."},"15":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-13","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#2-map-the-payload-fields","content":"Under **Field mappings**, edit `demo-bess` using the pencil button. For your own payload, select **Add mapping** instead."},"16":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-14","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#2-map-the-payload-fields","content":"Choose the asset type and set **Asset ID path** to the field containing the local device ID. In the example, `device.id` identifies the device, and `measurements.soc` supplies the FlexBIT field `bess_storage_soc`."},"17":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-15","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#2-map-the-payload-fields","content":"Enter a payload path for each measurement you want to send, then save. Mapping changes apply without restarting the adapter."},"18":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-16","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#2-map-the-payload-fields","content":"To add a value automatically to every event, change the field's source from **Payload path** to **Fixed value**, then enter its value. For example, set `bess_storage_energy_nominal` to `100` when that capacity stays the same for every message."},"19":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-17","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#2-map-the-payload-fields","content":"Fixed values accept plain text or JSON. Enter `100` for a number, `false` for a boolean, or `null` for null. Quote text such as `\"100\"` or `\"false\"` to keep it as a string. An empty fixed value adds an empty string. Payload paths and fixed values can be mixed in the same mapping."},"20":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-18","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#2-map-the-payload-fields","content":"In YAML, a fixed field uses `constant` and `has_constant: true` instead of `source`:"},"21":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-19","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#over-http","content":"Run this from the installation directory to send the BESS payload shown in the screenshots."},"22":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-20","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#over-http","content":"With Docker:"},"23":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-21","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#over-http","content":"Without Docker, use a second terminal while the adapter is running:"},"24":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-22","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#over-http","content":"For your own integration, POST a JSON object to `/v1/ingest` with `Content-Type: application/json` and `X-Mapping-Id` set to your mapping ID. For example:"},"25":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-23","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#over-http","content":"An `accepted` response means the adapter saved the event locally. Check **Events** for delivery to FlexBIT."},"26":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-24","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#over-mqtt","content":"Open **MQTT** and select **Add connection**. Enter your broker URL and client ID, add credentials if needed, and add a subscription pairing your telemetry topic with a mapping ID."},"27":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-25","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#over-mqtt","content":"For the bundled Docker broker, use `mqtt://mosquitto:1883`. For standalone mode, use the address of your external broker. A BESS subscription can use topic `devices/bess/telemetry` and mapping ID `demo-bess`."},"28":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-26","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#over-mqtt","content":"If you enabled MQTT during Docker installation, the local connection and demo subscriptions are already configured. With Mosquitto clients installed on the host, send a test message with:"},"29":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-27","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#assign-a-device-by-mqtt-topic","content":"When each device publishes to its own topic, the payload does not need a device ID:"},"30":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-28","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#assign-a-device-by-mqtt-topic","content":"Under **Field mappings**, edit your mapping and set **Asset ID source** to **MQTT topic**. Save the mapping."},"31":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-29","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#assign-a-device-by-mqtt-topic","content":"Under **Device mappings**, select **Add device mapping**. Enter the full incoming topic, for example `devices/bess/telemetry`, in **Local device ID or MQTT topic**, then select the remote platform device."},"32":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-30","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#assign-a-device-by-mqtt-topic","content":"On **MQTT**, use that field mapping's ID in the subscription for the topic."},"33":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-31","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#assign-a-device-by-mqtt-topic","content":"Each exact topic can point to a different platform device while sharing the same field mapping. A wildcard subscription such as `devices/+/telemetry` still needs a device mapping for each full incoming topic; device mappings do not match `+` or `#` filters."},"34":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-32","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#assign-a-device-by-mqtt-topic","content":"The YAML equivalent sets `asset_id_source: mqtt_topic` in the field mapping, replacing `asset_id_path`. Topic assignments use the existing `device_ids` table in `config/device-mappings.yaml`:"},"35":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-33","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#assign-a-device-by-mqtt-topic","content":"Without an assignment, the topic itself becomes the asset ID. Topic-based field mappings require MQTT events. Keep **Asset ID source** set to **Asset ID path** for HTTP intake or MQTT payloads that identify the device in a field. Existing mappings continue to use their asset ID path."},"36":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-34","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#assign-a-device-by-mqtt-topic","content":"For commands to a device, add a **Command target** linking its FlexBIT asset ID to an MQTT connection and topic. Check outgoing attempts on **Commands**. The current adapter accepts commands through `/internal/commands`; it does not subscribe to platform commands automatically."},"37":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-35","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#4-check-the-result","content":"Open **Events** and expand your event. The left side shows the incoming JSON; the right side shows the mapped FlexBIT payload and delivery state."},"38":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-36","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#4-check-the-result","content":"**Pending** means the event is waiting for mapping. Check its error, mapping ID, and payload paths if it stays pending."},"39":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-37","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#4-check-the-result","content":"**Mapped** means the payload is ready for delivery."},"40":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-38","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#4-check-the-result","content":"**Delivered** means the adapter sent it to FlexBIT."},"41":{"id":"/docs/how-it-works-39","page_id":"/docs/how-it-works","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/how-it-works#4-check-the-result","content":"If delivery does not complete, check the error shown on the event and verify your FlexBIT credentials and network connection."},"42":{"id":"/docs/installation","page_id":"/docs/installation","type":"page","content":"Installation","breadcrumbs":["FlexBIT Local Adapter"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/installation"},"43":{"id":"/docs/installation-0","page_id":"/docs/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/installation","content":"Install with Docker or run the standalone adapter on Linux."},"44":{"id":"/docs/installation-1","page_id":"/docs/installation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/installation#get-your-flexbit-credentials","content":"Get your FlexBIT credentials"},"45":{"id":"/docs/installation-2","page_id":"/docs/installation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/installation#run-the-installer","content":"Run the installer"},"46":{"id":"/docs/installation-3","page_id":"/docs/installation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/installation#with-docker","content":"With Docker"},"47":{"id":"/docs/installation-4","page_id":"/docs/installation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/installation#without-docker","content":"Without Docker"},"48":{"id":"/docs/installation-5","page_id":"/docs/installation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/installation#manage-the-adapter-from-a-terminal","content":"Manage the adapter from a terminal"},"49":{"id":"/docs/installation-6","page_id":"/docs/installation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/installation#update-an-existing-installation","content":"Update an existing installation"},"50":{"id":"/docs/installation-7","page_id":"/docs/installation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/installation#open-the-dashboard","content":"Open the dashboard"},"51":{"id":"/docs/installation-8","page_id":"/docs/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/installation#get-your-flexbit-credentials","content":"Open the connection page for your environment:"},"52":{"id":"/docs/installation-9","page_id":"/docs/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/installation#get-your-flexbit-credentials","content":"Testing"},"53":{"id":"/docs/installation-10","page_id":"/docs/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/installation#get-your-flexbit-credentials","content":"Production"},"54":{"id":"/docs/installation-11","page_id":"/docs/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/installation#get-your-flexbit-credentials","content":"Select your site, create an API key, and copy the **Site ID**, **API Key ID**, and **API Key Secret**. The secret is shown only once. The installer asks for these as Site ID, API ID, and API secret."},"55":{"id":"/docs/installation-12","page_id":"/docs/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/installation#run-the-installer","content":"Run these commands in a terminal:"},"56":{"id":"/docs/installation-13","page_id":"/docs/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/installation#run-the-installer","content":"The installer asks whether to use Docker. Choose yes for Docker or no for standalone Linux, or select a mode directly with the commands below."},"57":{"id":"/docs/installation-14","page_id":"/docs/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/installation#with-docker","content":"Install Docker with the Docker Compose plugin and start Docker. To select Docker directly, run:"},"58":{"id":"/docs/installation-15","page_id":"/docs/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/installation#with-docker","content":"Follow the prompts. Choose your FlexBIT environment, enter your credentials, and choose whether to start now. Optional services are:"},"59":{"id":"/docs/installation-16","page_id":"/docs/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/installation#with-docker","content":"**MQTT** adds a local Mosquitto broker for device telemetry."},"60":{"id":"/docs/installation-17","page_id":"/docs/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/installation#with-docker","content":"**History** adds QuestDB to store telemetry history."},"61":{"id":"/docs/installation-18","page_id":"/docs/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/installation#with-docker","content":"**Monitoring** adds Prometheus and Grafana."},"62":{"id":"/docs/installation-19","page_id":"/docs/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/installation#with-docker","content":"The installer downloads the image and starts the selected services. To check the adapter or stop it later, run these from the installation directory:"},"63":{"id":"/docs/installation-20","page_id":"/docs/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/installation#without-docker","content":"Use 64-bit Linux on AMD64 or ARM64, with glibc 2.28 or newer, libstdc++ 6.0.25 or newer, `curl`, `unzip`, and `sha256sum`. Raspberry Pi needs a 64-bit OS. Node is included in the package."},"64":{"id":"/docs/installation-21","page_id":"/docs/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/installation#without-docker","content":"Choose your environment and enter your credentials. Choose **yes** at the system service prompt to run in the background and start on boot. This requires systemd and root or sudo access. You can select it directly with:"},"65":{"id":"/docs/installation-22","page_id":"/docs/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/installation#without-docker","content":"Manage the service with:"},"66":{"id":"/docs/installation-23","page_id":"/docs/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/installation#without-docker","content":"Choose **no** at the service prompt to run in the terminal. Press Ctrl-C to stop; run `./start-adapter.sh` from the installation directory to start again. Standalone mode can connect to an external MQTT broker, but does not install the optional Docker services."},"67":{"id":"/docs/installation-24","page_id":"/docs/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/installation#manage-the-adapter-from-a-terminal","content":"Installation and update add the executable `flexbit-adapter` command. It is linked from `/usr/local/bin` by default, with sudo when needed. Choose a user-local directory with the installer's `--bin-dir \"$HOME/.local/bin\"` option and add that directory to `PATH`. Updates remember the chosen command directory."},"68":{"id":"/docs/installation-25","page_id":"/docs/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/installation#manage-the-adapter-from-a-terminal","content":"You can run these from any directory. Systemd installations use the existing service. Docker actions affect only the adapter, leaving optional services running. Uninstall removes the service or adapter container and keeps configuration, data, and the management command."},"69":{"id":"/docs/installation-26","page_id":"/docs/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/installation#manage-the-adapter-from-a-terminal","content":"For a foreground installation, `flexbit-adapter start` runs in the terminal and stops with Ctrl-C. Install with `--with-service` for background start/stop control. `flexbit-adapter --help` also shows command help. The executable is included in new release ZIPs; updating older installations adds it too."},"70":{"id":"/docs/installation-27","page_id":"/docs/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/installation#update-an-existing-installation","content":"Run this from the installation directory to update to the latest published release:"},"71":{"id":"/docs/installation-28","page_id":"/docs/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/installation#update-an-existing-installation","content":"Downloading the current installer first also supports older installations that have no update command. To select a release, add `--version X.Y.Z`. To update a different directory, add `--install-dir /path/to/installation`."},"72":{"id":"/docs/installation-29","page_id":"/docs/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/installation#update-an-existing-installation","content":"The installer detects Docker or standalone mode and preserves your configuration, credentials, and data. It does not ask for credentials again. Docker updates change the adapter image in `.env` and recreate only the adapter; optional services keep running. Standalone updates verify the downloaded ZIP and bundled Node runtime before replacing application files. A systemd installation stops and restarts its existing service without changing the unit."},"73":{"id":"/docs/installation-30","page_id":"/docs/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/installation#update-an-existing-installation","content":"For a foreground installation, stop the adapter with Ctrl-C before updating. The new version starts in the foreground when the update finishes. Add `--no-start` to leave a standalone installation stopped after the update. For Docker, `--no-start` saves the new image setting but leaves the running container unchanged; use the recap's start command to recreate it later."},"74":{"id":"/docs/installation-31","page_id":"/docs/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/installation#open-the-dashboard","content":"Open http\\://127.0.0.1:4000 on the machine running the adapter. The installer prints the dashboard URL and any optional service links."},"75":{"id":"/docs/installation-32","page_id":"/docs/installation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/installation#open-the-dashboard","content":"Continue with How it works to map your device and send your first event."}},"count":75},"sorting":{"language":"english","sortableProperties":["content","page_id","type","url"],"sortablePropertiesWithTypes":{"content":"string","page_id":"string","type":"string","url":"string"},"sorts":{"content":{"docs":{"1":28,"2":38,"3":6,"4":7,"5":8,"6":46,"7":47,"8":11,"9":9,"10":55,"11":67,"12":41,"13":25,"14":56,"15":63,"16":13,"17":18,"18":61,"19":19,"20":31,"21":51,"22":70,"23":73,"24":26,"25":10,"26":42,"27":24,"28":30,"29":68,"30":64,"31":62,"32":39,"33":17,"34":60,"35":71,"36":23,"37":40,"38":5,"39":2,"40":0,"41":29,"42":34,"43":33,"44":27,"45":49,"46":69,"47":72,"48":36,"49":65,"50":45,"51":44,"52":54,"53":48,"54":53,"55":50,"56":57,"57":32,"58":20,"59":4,"60":1,"61":3,"62":59,"63":66,"64":14,"65":37,"66":12,"67":35,"68":74,"69":21,"70":52,"71":16,"72":58,"73":22,"74":43,"75":15},"orderedDocs":[[40,"**Delivered** means the adapter sent it to FlexBIT."],[60,"**History** adds QuestDB to store telemetry history."],[39,"**Mapped** means the payload is ready for delivery."],[61,"**Monitoring** adds Prometheus and Grafana."],[59,"**MQTT** adds a local Mosquitto broker for device telemetry."],[38,"**Pending** means the event is waiting for mapping. Check its error, mapping ID, and payload paths if it stays pending."],[3,"1\\. Connect your device to a FlexBIT asset"],[4,"2\\. Map the payload fields"],[5,"3\\. Send telemetry"],[9,"4\\. Check the result"],[25,"An `accepted` response means the adapter saved the event locally. Check **Events** for delivery to FlexBIT."],[8,"Assign a device by MQTT topic"],[66,"Choose **no** at the service prompt to run in the terminal. Press Ctrl-C to stop; run `./start-adapter.sh` from the installation directory to start again. Standalone mode can connect to an external MQTT broker, but does not install the optional Docker services."],[16,"Choose the asset type and set **Asset ID path** to the field containing the local device ID. In the example, `device.id` identifies the device, and `measurements.soc` supplies the FlexBIT field `bess_storage_soc`."],[64,"Choose your environment and enter your credentials. Choose **yes** at the system service prompt to run in the background and start on boot. This requires systemd and root or sudo access. You can select it directly with:"],[75,"Continue with How it works to map your device and send your first event."],[71,"Downloading the current installer first also supports older installations that have no update command. To select a release, add `--version X.Y.Z`. To update a different directory, add `--install-dir /path/to/installation`."],[33,"Each exact topic can point to a different platform device while sharing the same field mapping. A wildcard subscription such as `devices/+/telemetry` still needs a device mapping for each full incoming topic; device mappings do not match `+` or `#` filters."],[17,"Enter a payload path for each measurement you want to send, then save. Mapping changes apply without restarting the adapter."],[19,"Fixed values accept plain text or JSON. Enter `100` for a number, `false` for a boolean, or `null` for null. Quote text such as `\"100\"` or `\"false\"` to keep it as a string. An empty fixed value adds an empty string. Payload paths and fixed values can be mixed in the same mapping."],[58,"Follow the prompts. Choose your FlexBIT environment, enter your credentials, and choose whether to start now. Optional services are:"],[69,"For a foreground installation, `flexbit-adapter start` runs in the terminal and stops with Ctrl-C. Install with `--with-service` for background start/stop control. `flexbit-adapter --help` also shows command help. The executable is included in new release ZIPs; updating older installations adds it too."],[73,"For a foreground installation, stop the adapter with Ctrl-C before updating. The new version starts in the foreground when the update finishes. Add `--no-start` to leave a standalone installation stopped after the update. For Docker, `--no-start` saves the new image setting but leaves the running container unchanged; use the recap's start command to recreate it later."],[36,"For commands to a device, add a **Command target** linking its FlexBIT asset ID to an MQTT connection and topic. Check outgoing attempts on **Commands**. The current adapter accepts commands through `/internal/commands`; it does not subscribe to platform commands automatically."],[27,"For the bundled Docker broker, use `mqtt://mosquitto:1883`. For standalone mode, use the address of your external broker. A BESS subscription can use topic `devices/bess/telemetry` and mapping ID `demo-bess`."],[13,"For this example, use `demo-bess` as the local device ID and select a BESS asset from your site. This tells the adapter which FlexBIT asset should receive the device's measurements."],[24,"For your own integration, POST a JSON object to `/v1/ingest` with `Content-Type: application/json` and `X-Mapping-Id` set to your mapping ID. For example:"],[44,"Get your FlexBIT credentials"],[1,"How it works"],[41,"If delivery does not complete, check the error shown on the event and verify your FlexBIT credentials and network connection."],[28,"If you enabled MQTT during Docker installation, the local connection and demo subscriptions are already configured. With Mosquitto clients installed on the host, send a test message with:"],[20,"In YAML, a fixed field uses `constant` and `has_constant: true` instead of `source`:"],[57,"Install Docker with the Docker Compose plugin and start Docker. To select Docker directly, run:"],[43,"Install with Docker or run the standalone adapter on Linux."],[42,"Installation"],[67,"Installation and update add the executable `flexbit-adapter` command. It is linked from `/usr/local/bin` by default, with sudo when needed. Choose a user-local directory with the installer's `--bin-dir \"$HOME/.local/bin\"` option and add that directory to `PATH`. Updates remember the chosen command directory."],[48,"Manage the adapter from a terminal"],[65,"Manage the service with:"],[2,"Map your device, send telemetry, and check delivery to FlexBIT."],[32,"On **MQTT**, use that field mapping's ID in the subscription for the topic."],[37,"Open **Events** and expand your event. The left side shows the incoming JSON; the right side shows the mapped FlexBIT payload and delivery state."],[12,"Open **Mappings**. Under **Device mappings**, select **Add device mapping**, enter your local device ID, and select the destination FlexBIT asset."],[26,"Open **MQTT** and select **Add connection**. Enter your broker URL and client ID, add credentials if needed, and add a subscription pairing your telemetry topic with a mapping ID."],[74,"Open http\\://127.0.0.1:4000 on the machine running the adapter. The installer prints the dashboard URL and any optional service links."],[51,"Open the connection page for your environment:"],[50,"Open the dashboard"],[6,"Over HTTP"],[7,"Over MQTT"],[53,"Production"],[45,"Run the installer"],[55,"Run these commands in a terminal:"],[21,"Run this from the installation directory to send the BESS payload shown in the screenshots."],[70,"Run this from the installation directory to update to the latest published release:"],[54,"Select your site, create an API key, and copy the **Site ID**, **API Key ID**, and **API Key Secret**. The secret is shown only once. The installer asks for these as Site ID, API ID, and API secret."],[52,"Testing"],[10,"The adapter receives JSON over HTTP or MQTT, saves it locally, maps its fields, and sends it to FlexBIT. If delivery fails, it keeps the event and retries."],[14,"The demo mappings shown here are included with the installation. Replace the example device mappings with your own asset selections."],[56,"The installer asks whether to use Docker. Choose yes for Docker or no for standalone Linux, or select a mode directly with the commands below."],[72,"The installer detects Docker or standalone mode and preserves your configuration, credentials, and data. It does not ask for credentials again. Docker updates change the adapter image in `.env` and recreate only the adapter; optional services keep running. Standalone updates verify the downloaded ZIP and bundled Node runtime before replacing application files. A systemd installation stops and restarts its existing service without changing the unit."],[62,"The installer downloads the image and starts the selected services. To check the adapter or stop it later, run these from the installation directory:"],[34,"The YAML equivalent sets `asset_id_source: mqtt_topic` in the field mapping, replacing `asset_id_path`. Topic assignments use the existing `device_ids` table in `config/device-mappings.yaml`:"],[18,"To add a value automatically to every event, change the field's source from **Payload path** to **Fixed value**, then enter its value. For example, set `bess_storage_energy_nominal` to `100` when that capacity stays the same for every message."],[31,"Under **Device mappings**, select **Add device mapping**. Enter the full incoming topic, for example `devices/bess/telemetry`, in **Local device ID or MQTT topic**, then select the remote platform device."],[15,"Under **Field mappings**, edit `demo-bess` using the pencil button. For your own payload, select **Add mapping** instead."],[30,"Under **Field mappings**, edit your mapping and set **Asset ID source** to **MQTT topic**. Save the mapping."],[49,"Update an existing installation"],[63,"Use 64-bit Linux on AMD64 or ARM64, with glibc 2.28 or newer, libstdc++ 6.0.25 or newer, `curl`, `unzip`, and `sha256sum`. Raspberry Pi needs a 64-bit OS. Node is included in the package."],[11,"Use the BESS example below to try the full flow. The same steps apply to other asset types."],[29,"When each device publishes to its own topic, the payload does not need a device ID:"],[46,"With Docker"],[22,"With Docker:"],[35,"Without an assignment, the topic itself becomes the asset ID. Topic-based field mappings require MQTT events. Keep **Asset ID source** set to **Asset ID path** for HTTP intake or MQTT payloads that identify the device in a field. Existing mappings continue to use their asset ID path."],[47,"Without Docker"],[23,"Without Docker, use a second terminal while the adapter is running:"],[68,"You can run these from any directory. Systemd installations use the existing service. Docker actions affect only the adapter, leaving optional services running. Uninstall removes the service or adapter container and keeps configuration, data, and the management command."]],"type":"string"},"page_id":{"docs":{"1":0,"2":1,"3":2,"4":3,"5":4,"6":5,"7":6,"8":7,"9":8,"10":9,"11":10,"12":11,"13":12,"14":13,"15":14,"16":15,"17":16,"18":17,"19":18,"20":19,"21":20,"22":21,"23":22,"24":23,"25":24,"26":25,"27":26,"28":27,"29":28,"30":29,"31":30,"32":31,"33":32,"34":33,"35":34,"36":35,"37":36,"38":37,"39":38,"40":39,"41":40,"42":41,"43":42,"44":43,"45":44,"46":45,"47":46,"48":47,"49":48,"50":49,"51":50,"52":51,"53":52,"54":53,"55":54,"56":55,"57":56,"58":57,"59":58,"60":59,"61":60,"62":61,"63":62,"64":63,"65":64,"66":65,"67":66,"68":67,"69":68,"70":69,"71":70,"72":71,"73":72,"74":73,"75":74},"orderedDocs":[[1,"/docs/how-it-works"],[2,"/docs/how-it-works"],[3,"/docs/how-it-works"],[4,"/docs/how-it-works"],[5,"/d